/**
 * A growable, contiguous, **owning** sequence of one element type.
 *
 * `mem::alloc<T>(n)` gives you memory; this gives you an owner. It knows how many elements it
 * holds, it knows how much room it has, it grows when you need more, and when its owner goes out of
 * scope the buffer goes with it. That one difference is what the whole type is.
 *
 * Deliberately **not** shaped like `string`. A string is a window plus a reference-counted buffer,
 * so copying one is a retain and a substring shares its parent's bytes. An array is a value you
 * own: `$b = $a` duplicates the elements, because that is the honest default for a value type and
 * anything cheaper would alias silently. Borrow it with `&$a` when the callee only reads, `mv` it
 * when you are handing it over, and reach for `->clone()` when you want the copy to be visible at
 * the call site rather than implied by an `=`.
 *
 * **It owns its elements, whatever they are.** An `array<string>` copies by copying each string, and
 * destroys each of them when it goes; an `array<int32>` copies with one `memcpy` and has nothing to
 * destroy. Both are this file, and the difference between them is `mem::is_trivially_copyable<T>()`
 * and `mem::needs_destruction<T>()` - the compiler's own answers about `T`, asked out loud, so a
 * nested owner is *correct* rather than a bitwise duplicate with no diagnostic anywhere.
 *
 * The one thing to know about the fast path: it is a fast path, not a rule. Both arms of every branch
 * below are correct for every `T`. The branch is there to buy back the `memcpy` for element types that
 * do not need the loop, and nothing about the type's behaviour depends on which arm ran.
 *
 * An element type that owns something and has *not* said how it is copied - a destructor and no copy
 * constructor - makes copying the array a located error rather than a silent bit copy. That is
 * `AST::classify_copy`'s refusal arriving here through the element assignment, and it is the answer:
 * the array cannot invent a copy its element type declined to define. Such an array is still built,
 * appended to, iterated and destroyed; it just cannot be duplicated.
 *
 * Counts, capacities and indices are `usize` throughout, matching `mem::`. There is no allocator
 * parameter and deliberately none yet: mem::alloc is allocator-free and Echo has no function
 * pointers, so a per-container allocator would be an invented ABI. The intended future shape is a
 * monomorphized array<T, A> plus mem::alloc_with, which is purely additive.
 *
 * `#[core: array]` is what lets the compiler *say* `array<E>`, and it needs to say it in exactly
 * one situation: `$a = [1, 2, 3];`, an array literal with no declared type to take one from. It
 * knows no method, no property and no shape of this type - everything below is looked up the way
 * your own code is.
 */
#[core: array]
public struct array<T> : contract::iterable<T>, contract::const_iterable<const T>
{
    // **the allocation, and the length that says how much of it is live.** two fields and not three,
    // because the address and the capacity are one thing - they are created together, they move
    // together, they are freed together, and neither means anything without the other.
    //
    // `mem::buffer<T>` is `#[unique]`, so exactly one array names this allocation and the compiler knows
    // it. what that buys is a fact no type-based rule could give: two live arrays are two buffers,
    // even when both are `array<usize>`. `$len` stays here because how much of the buffer is live is
    // *this* type's business - a buffer has a capacity and deliberately no length
    mem::buffer<T> $storage;
    usize $len;

    /**
     * An empty array that has not allocated anything.
     *
     * A null buffer is a legitimate state and every method below is written for it: `mem::realloc`
     * over a null pointer allocates, and `mem::free` over one is a no-op. So there is no
     * "uninitialized" case separate from "empty".
     */
    constructor()
    {
        $this->storage = mem::buffer<T>();
        $this->len = 0;
    }

    /**
     * The deep copy. Recognised rather than declared - any constructor taking one non-nullable
     * borrow of its own type is the copy constructor (`AST::is_copy_constructor`), which is what
     * makes `$b = $a` and `array<int32>($a)` the same operation.
     *
     * Written out because a declared destructor disqualifies the synthesized copy, and rightly so:
     * a bitwise duplicate of a struct holding a heap pointer is two owners of one buffer.
     *
     * Spelled `array<T>&`, not `array&`. Inside a generic struct the bare name is the *template*,
     * which is not a type; only the applied form matches.
     *
     * And `const`, because a copy reads its source and nothing more. That is what lets `$b = $a` and
     * `$a->clone()` work when `$a` is `const` - without it the copy is the one operation a const
     * array cannot take part in, including the one its own `clone()` is written through.
     *
     * It is also what `extend`'s element loop stands on, and that direction is worth naming: `$other[$i]`
     * through a `const array<T>&` is a `const T&`, so copying it needs the element type's own copy to
     * accept a const source. The compiler synthesizes one that does wherever the properties allow it
     * (`AST::copy_source_may_be_const`) - so an element type that declares a *mutable* copy constructor
     * by hand is one this array cannot copy, which is that author's decision arriving here rather than
     * a limitation of this file.
     *
     * **Written through `extend`**, the way the destructor is written through `clear()` and `push`
     * through `$a[] =`: appending every element of another array *is* what a copy does, so the
     * `const if`, the bulk `mem::copy` and the element loop have one implementation rather than two
     * that have to agree about the hardest rule in this file.
     */
    constructor(const array<T>& $other)
    {
        $this->storage = mem::buffer<T>();
        $this->len = 0;

        // sized to the byte and not through `reserve`, because a copy is not an array still growing:
        // the floor and the doubling exist for a run of appends, and this is one allocation for a
        // known count. `reserve_exact(0)` returns without allocating, so an empty source still copies
        // to an empty array rather than to a zero-length allocation - and `extend` then has nothing
        // left to reserve, so this is the only allocation on either path
        $this->reserve_exact($other->count());

        $this->extend($other);
    }

    /**
     * Destroys every element, then releases the buffer. Runs at the end of the scope that owns the
     * array, once.
     *
     * Written *through* `clear()`, the way `push` is written through `$a[] =` and `iterate` through
     * `sub()`: what it means to destroy the elements is one implementation rather than two that could
     * drift.
     *
     * **The buffer is not freed here**, and that split is the point: this body destroys *values*, and
     * `mem::buffer<T>`'s own destructor - which runs straight after this one, as every owning property's
     * does - releases the *storage*. An array owns elements; a buffer owns memory. Neither has to know
     * the other's half.
     */
    destructor()
    {
        $this->clear();
    }

    /**
     * How many elements are in it.
     */
    const function count() : usize
    {
        return $this->len;
    }

    /**
     * How many it has room for before it has to grow.
     */
    const function capacity() : usize
    {
        return $this->storage->capacity();
    }

    /**
     * True when it holds no elements. Says nothing about capacity.
     */
    const function is_empty() : bool
    {
        if ($this->len > 0) {
            return false;
        }

        return true;
    }

    /**
     * Makes room for at least `$count` elements without changing `count()`.
     *
     * Doubling rather than fitting exactly, so a run of appends costs an amortized constant each
     * instead of being O(n²). Call it up front when you already know the size - that is the one
     * allocation for the whole loop, because a request above the floor below is honoured exactly.
     *
     * Use `reserve_exact()` when you want a small array sized to the byte.
     *
     * **This may move the buffer**, so every borrow into the array is stale afterwards: every
     * `slice<T>` you took, every `&$a[$i]`. See book/concept/arrays.md, "Growth and invalidation".
     */
    function reserve(usize $count) : void
    {
        // read once: `capacity()` is an ordinary call, and on the JIT path - which runs no IR pass -
        // asking twice is two real calls on every append that reaches here
        usize $cap = $this->storage->capacity();

        if ($cap >= $count) {
            return;
        }

        usize $grown = $cap * 2;

        if ($grown < $count) {
            $grown = $count;
        }

        // **a floor on the first allocation.** doubling from zero is zero, so without this the clamp
        // above hands back exactly `$count`, and an array filled one element at a time reallocates on
        // its first, second *and* third push to arrive at four elements - three trips through the
        // allocator to do one allocation's work.
        //
        // it can only ever raise `$grown`, and `$grown` is already max(cap * 2, $count) - so a request
        // above the floor is untouched and the doc's promise about reserving up front is unchanged.
        // `reserve_exact` is the way to ask for a small buffer sized to the byte.
        //
        // by element size, which is what Rust's RawVec calls MIN_NON_ZERO_CAP: the smaller the element,
        // the more of them fit in the smallest block an allocator will hand out at all, so the floor
        // costs nothing there and saves more. A `T` bigger than a page gets no floor, because for one of
        // those the allocation *is* the cost and rounding it up doubles that.
        //
        // plain `if`s and not `const if`: `mem::size_of<T>()` folds to a constant at the call site in
        // codegen, so both tests are already decided by the time LLVM sees them - but AST::const_fold
        // cannot answer a layout query before codegen, so `const if` would refuse it. When that
        // lands these should become `const if` and stop being branches in an unoptimized build too
        usize $floor = 4;

        if (mem::size_of<T>() == 1) {
            $floor = 8;
        }

        if (mem::size_of<T>() > 1024) {
            $floor = 1;
        }

        if ($grown < $floor) {
            $grown = $floor;
        }

        // re-seated through `:$`, because realloc hands back an address rather than writing through
        // the old one
        //
        // and a bitwise move of the existing elements, which is correct for an owning `T` and not the
        // hole the copy constructor's `mem::copy` was: nothing is *duplicated* here. each element is
        // relocated, the old block is gone, and the count of owners is unchanged - so there is no copy
        // to run and none to skip
        $this->storage->resize($grown);
    }

    /**
     * Makes room for exactly `$count` elements - no doubling, no floor.
     *
     * For the array you will fill once and keep: `reserve()` may hand back more than you asked for, and
     * for a long-lived array holding a known number of elements that slack is memory nobody reclaims.
     *
     * The trade is the one its name implies. Filling past `$count` afterwards reallocates immediately,
     * because there is no headroom left to absorb it - so this is the wrong call for an array that is
     * still growing, and `reserve()` is the right one.
     */
    function reserve_exact(usize $count) : void
    {
        if ($this->storage->capacity() >= $count) {
            return;
        }

        $this->storage->resize($count);
    }

    /**
     * Gives back everything past `count()`, so `capacity()` becomes `count()`.
     *
     * The other half of what `reserve` and `pop` leave behind: nothing else in this type ever lowers
     * `cap`, so an array that grew large and then emptied holds its buffer until it is destroyed.
     *
     * **This may move the buffer**, exactly as `reserve` may - every borrow into the array is stale
     * afterwards.
     */
    function shrink_to_fit() : void
    {
        if ($this->storage->capacity() == $this->len) {
            return;
        }

        // `mem::realloc` over a zero count is the same shape as `mem::free` plus a null, and `$data` may
        // legitimately end up null here - which every method in this type is already written for
        $this->storage->resize($this->len);
    }

    /**
     * Appends one element. Exactly what `$a[] = $value` means, written as a call.
     *
     * The body *is* `$a[] = $value`, so the growth, the fresh-slot rule and the append itself have
     * one implementation rather than two that could drift.
     */
    function push(T $value) : void
    {
        $this[] = $value;
    }

    /**
     * Appends every element of `$other`, leaving `$other` untouched.
     *
     * **One allocation and, for most element types, one `memcpy`.** Reserving up front is what makes it
     * one allocation; the `const if` below is what makes it one copy. Appending in a loop instead costs
     * a capacity re-check and a growth test per element, and for a `T` that copies as bytes it costs
     * that instead of nothing at all.
     *
     * **`$a->extend($a)` is refused**, and that is a rule rather than an oversight: `$this` takes
     * `inout` access by being a method's receiver, `$other` takes `read` by being a const borrow, and
     * one array cannot be both at once. Neither word is written here - see
     * book/concept/pointers_and_refs_v2.md, "Addresses may alias, accesses may not conflict".
     * `extend_from_within` below is how you append an array to itself.
     */
    function extend(const array<T>& $other) : void
    {
        // read once, not per use and not per iteration: `$other` is a `const` borrow so nothing here can
        // change what it answers, and the loop below would otherwise pay a call per element on the `run`
        // path, which performs no IR passes at all
        usize $n = $other->count();

        // an empty source appends nothing, and reserving for it would round a zero-length request up to
        // the floor - allocating a buffer for a call that was asked to do nothing
        if ($n == 0) {
            return;
        }

        $this->reserve($this->len + $n);

        // **one branch, two correct answers**, and the copy constructor above is written through this:
        // a `T` that copies as bytes copies in bulk, and one that does not is copied an element at a
        // time so each gets whatever its own copy means - a retain for a class, a copy constructor for
        // a struct that declares one.
        //
        // the loop is not the fallback and the memcpy is not the rule: both arms produce the same
        // array. what the condition buys is the bulk copy for the element types that can take one,
        // which is most of them.
        //
        // `const if` and not `if`, so the arm this `T` does not use is not compiled - an `array<int32>`
        // used to carry the element loop's blocks and the `operator []` instantiation they needed,
        // behind a branch that could not be taken
        const if (mem::is_trivially_copyable<T>()) {
            $this->storage->copy_from($this->len, $other->storage, 0, $n);

            // written after the copy, not before: `len` is what says which slots are live, and between
            // the two statements the new ones are not yet
            $this->len = $this->len + $n;
        }
        else {
            usize $i = 0;

            while ($i < $n) {
                // `$this[]` and not an indexed write, for the reason the copy constructor gives: the
                // append is the one element-write the compiler reads as an *initialization*, so the
                // uninitialized slot it just grew is not torn down before being written
                $this[] = $other[$i];

                $i = $i + 1;
            }
        }
    }

    /**
     * Appends `$count` elements taken from this array's own range starting at `$start`.
     *
     * The self-append, spelled explicitly. `$a->extend($a)` is refused because one array cannot be
     * both the `inout` receiver and a `read` source of one call; this is the same intent said in the
     * one way that is unambiguous, and it is a better API besides - it says *which* range to repeat
     * rather than "all of it, as it was when you asked".
     *
     *     $a->extend_from_within(0, $a->count());   // double it
     *     $a->extend_from_within(0, 1);             // repeat the first element
     *
     * The two ranges live in one allocation and are **statically disjoint**: the destination begins at
     * the old length and the source ends at or before it. That is a fact about ranges rather than
     * about types, which is exactly the kind a type-based aliasing rule could never express.
     */
    function extend_from_within(usize $start, usize $count) : void
    {
        // two separate checks rather than one on the sum, so the sum cannot wrap on the way to being
        // checked - the same shape `sub` uses, and for the same reason
        assert($start <= $this->len, "extend_from_within start out of range");
        assert($count <= $this->len - $start, "extend_from_within count out of range");

        if ($count == 0) {
            return;
        }

        usize $old_len = $this->len;

        // **one reserve, up front, and it is load-bearing rather than an optimisation.** after it the
        // buffer cannot move again for the rest of this call - which is what makes the source range
        // safe to read *while* the destination range is being filled. without it the append in the
        // element loop below could reallocate mid-way and leave the source borrow pointing at the
        // block that was just freed
        $this->reserve($old_len + $count);

        // the same one-branch-two-answers split `extend` uses, and the same reason for each arm
        const if (mem::is_trivially_copyable<T>()) {
            // `mem::copy` and not `mem::move`: its two regions must not overlap, and here they
            // provably do not - the source ends at `$start + $count <= $old_len`, which is where the
            // destination begins
            $this->storage->copy_nonoverlapping($old_len, $start, $count);

            // after the copy, for `extend`'s reason: between the two statements the new slots are not
            // yet live
            $this->len = $old_len + $count;
        }
        else {
            usize $i = 0;

            while ($i < $count) {
                // reading `$this[$start + $i]` while appending to `$this` is safe for exactly one
                // reason, and it is the reserve above: the read is of a slot below `$old_len`, the
                // write is of a fresh slot above it, and no reallocation can move either
                $this[] = $this[$start + $i];

                $i = $i + 1;
            }
        }
    }

    /**
     * Grows by one and hands back a borrow of the new element, so it can be filled in place.
     *
     * For a value that has to be built field by field, which is the case a single expression cannot
     * cover. `&$a[]` is the same thing written as a place, and reads better at most call sites.
     *
     * The slot starts out **uninitialized** while `count()` has already grown, so filling it is
     * part of the contract rather than an option.
     */
    function push_slot() : T&
    {
        return &$this[];
    }

    /**
     * Removes the last element and hands over its ownership.
     *
     * Hands over, and does not duplicate: the caller receives the element the array was holding, and
     * the array is no longer holding it. For an element type that owns something that is the whole
     * difference between this and reading `$a[$a->count() - 1]`, which copies.
     */
    function pop() : T
    {
        assert($this->len > 0, "pop from an empty array");

        // shortened first, so the slot is already outside `len` when its value is taken. between these
        // two statements nothing else may look at this array, and nothing does
        $this->len = $this->len - 1;

        // reading the slot here would be a *copy* - the element is a place, so the ownership pass gives
        // it a retain or a copy constructor, and the slot it came from keeps the claim it always had.
        // for a `string` that is a reference nothing will ever release, once per pop
        return mem::take<T>($this->storage->at($this->len));
    }

    /**
     * Removes the element at `$index` and hands over its ownership, **shifting everything after it down**.
     * Preserves order.
     *
     * O(n), which is the whole difference from `swap_remove` below - reach for that one when order does not
     * matter and this one when it does. `ordered_map<K, V>` is what this exists for: it keeps its keys in
     * insertion order, so removing one has to close the gap without reordering the rest.
     */
    function remove(usize $index) : T
    {
        assert($index < $this->len, "remove index out of range");

        // taken first and unconditionally, for swap_remove's reason: it keeps the single `return` outside
        // every branch, and an owning local moved out of on one arm and not the other is a conditional move
        T $doomed = mem::take<T>($this->storage->at($index));

        // **a bitwise relocation of the tail**, and deliberately not a loop of indexed writes: an indexed
        // write is a *re*-assignment, so the ownership pass would end whatever the destination held - and
        // the destination is the slot the element above it is about to move out of. Nothing is duplicated
        // here, each element simply moves down one slot, the last one falls outside `len`, and the count of
        // owners is unchanged.
        //
        // `mem::move` and not `mem::copy`: the two regions overlap by every slot but one, which is exactly
        // what `copy` promises they do not
        if ($index + 1 < $this->len) {
            $this->storage->move($index, $index + 1, $this->len - $index - 1);
        }

        $this->len = $this->len - 1;

        return $doomed;
    }

    /**
     * Removes the element at `$index` and hands over its ownership, moving the last element into the
     * hole. **Does not preserve order.**
     *
     * O(1), which is the entire reason it exists: it is the removal that does not shift everything after
     * the index down. When order does not matter - a working set, a free list, anything being filtered -
     * this is the one to reach for.
     *
     * The last element is not a special case: removing it is the whole operation with the relocation
     * skipped, so there is one `mem::take` and one `return` on every path.
     */
    function swap_remove(usize $index) : T
    {
        assert($index < $this->len, "swap_remove index out of range");

        // taken rather than read, so the array stops holding it and the caller starts - a read would
        // copy, leaving the slot owning a second reference nothing releases. taken *first* and
        // unconditionally, which is what keeps the single `return` below outside any branch: an owning
        // local moved out of on one arm and not the other is a conditional move, and the ownership pass
        // says so
        T $doomed = mem::take<T>($this->storage->at($index));

        // the hole is filled from the end - unless it *is* the end, and the guard is not merely a
        // shortcut: `mem::copy`'s two regions must not overlap, and for the last element they are one
        // slot.
        //
        // **a bitwise relocation, and deliberately not `$this[$index] = ...`.** an indexed write is a
        // *re*-assignment, so the ownership pass would release whatever the destination held - and what
        // it holds is the slot `mem::take` has just emptied. this is `reserve`'s argument at the scale of
        // one element: nothing is duplicated, the last slot is about to fall outside `len`, and the count
        // of owners is unchanged, so there is no copy to run and none to skip
        if ($index + 1 < $this->len) {
            $this->storage->copy_nonoverlapping($index, $this->len - 1, 1);
        }

        $this->len = $this->len - 1;

        return $doomed;
    }

    /**
     * A copy of the element at `$index`, bounds-checked in every build.
     *
     * `$a[$index]` is the same element without the copy, and is what to reach for.
     */
    const function get(usize $index) : T
    {
        return $this[$index];
    }

    /**
     * A borrow of the element at `$index`. The method form of `$a[$index]`.
     *
     * Written *through* the element operator, the way `push()` is written through the append one:
     * the bounds rule and its message then have one implementation rather than two that could drift
     * apart, and `$a->at($i)` cannot come to mean something `$a[$i]` does not.
     */
    function at(usize $index) : T&
    {
        return &$this[$index];
    }

    /**
     * The same borrow, read-only, for when the array itself is `const`.
     *
     * The two are one overload set told apart by the receiver: `$a->at($i)` on a mutable array picks
     * the borrow above because its receiver matches exactly, and on a `const` one only this reaches.
     * Written through the const element operator for the reason the mutable form is written through
     * the mutable one.
     */
    const function at(usize $index) : const T&
    {
        return &$this[$index];
    }

    /**
     * Destroys every element and keeps the buffer, so the next fill reuses the allocation.
     *
     * `count()` goes to zero and `capacity()` does not move. For an element type that owns something
     * each element is destroyed here, at this call, rather than being forgotten about - forgetting is
     * what leaks it, since the buffer is raw storage nothing else walks.
     *
     * This is where the array's element teardown lives; the destructor is this plus the `mem::free`.
     */
    function clear() : void
    {
        $this->truncate(0);
    }

    /**
     * Destroys every element past `$count` and keeps the buffer.
     *
     * `count()` becomes `$count` - or stays where it is, if it was already that short. `capacity()` does
     * not move; `shrink_to_fit()` is what gives the buffer back.
     *
     * This is where the array's element teardown lives. `clear()` is this with a bound of zero, and the
     * destructor is `clear()` plus the `mem::free` - so what it means to destroy a run of elements has one
     * implementation rather than three that could drift.
     */
    function truncate(usize $count) : void
    {
        // shortening to something longer than it already is means nothing, and must not *grow* `len` -
        // which the loop below would not do, but the final store would
        if ($count >= $this->len) {
            return;
        }

        // nothing to run for an element type that owns nothing, and the guard is what keeps that free:
        // without it an `array<int32>` walks the elements it is dropping to do nothing to each of them
        //
        // `const if`, so "free" is literal rather than nearly: there is no guard in the emitted code and no
        // loop behind it. `array<int32>::truncate` is the one store `count()` promises, and so is `clear`
        const if (mem::needs_destruction<T>()) {
            // counting down and shortening as it goes, so `len` always says exactly which slots are
            // still live. that matters if a `T`'s destructor ever reaches back into this array: it sees
            // a shorter, consistent array rather than one whose elements have already been taken
            //
            // `mem::take` and not a read: a read *copies*, which would leave the slot holding a second
            // owner nothing will ever release. this hands the element over and leaves the storage dead,
            // which is exactly what `len` above has just claimed
            //
            // `$doomed` is a local of this loop's body, so it is destroyed at the end of each iteration
            // by the ordinary scope rule. there is no drop written here and none to keep in step
            while ($this->len > $count) {
                $this->len = $this->len - 1;

                T $doomed = mem::take<T>($this->storage->at($this->len));
            }
        }

        $this->len = $count;
    }

    /**
     * A borrowed window over everything.
     *
     * Written out rather than implicit. An `array<T>` does not silently decay to a `slice<T>` at a
     * call site - the conversion allocates nothing, but it does hand out a borrow, and handing out
     * a borrow is a decision you should see. That is a choice, not a limitation.
     */
    function sub() : slice<T>
    {
        return $this->sub(0, $this->len);
    }

    /**
     * The same window over a `const` array: read-only, and said in the window's own type.
     */
    const function sub() : slice<const T>
    {
        return $this->sub(0, $this->len);
    }

    /**
     * A borrowed window over `$count` elements from `$from`.
     *
     * Named `sub` rather than `slice` to match `slice<T>::sub` and `string::view::sub` - one word for
     * "a narrower window over the same bytes", wherever you ask for one.
     */
    function sub(usize $from, usize $count) : slice<T>
    {
        assert($from <= $this->len, "slice start out of range");
        assert($count <= $this->len - $from, "slice length out of range");

        return $this->storage->sub($from, $count);
    }

    /**
     * The same narrower window, read-only - `at`'s pairing, one level down.
     */
    const function sub(usize $from, usize $count) : slice<const T>
    {
        assert($from <= $this->len, "slice start out of range");
        assert($count <= $this->len - $from, "slice length out of range");

        return $this->storage->sub($from, $count);
    }

    /**
     * A cursor over the elements.
     *
     * Written *through* `->sub()` so the window rules have one implementation and this cannot drift
     * from what `$a->sub()` hands out.
     */
    function iterate() : slice_iterator<T>
    {
        return slice_iterator<T>($this->sub());
    }

    /**
     * A cursor over the elements of an array nobody may write.
     *
     * The read-only half of the protocol - `contract::const_iterable<const T>` above - and it is what
     * makes `foreach` over a `const` array work. Written through the const `sub()` for the mutable
     * one's reason, and needing no cursor of its own: `slice_iterator<const T>` falls out of
     * substitution, and its `current()` hands back `const T&`.
     */
    const function iterate() : slice_iterator<const T>
    {
        return slice_iterator<const T>($this->sub());
    }

    /**
     * An explicit deep copy - the same thing `$b = $a` does, said out loud at the call site.
     */
    const function clone() : array<T>
    {
        return array<T>($this);
    }
}

/**
 * The element contract. `$a[$i]` reads, writes, takes `&` and chains with `->`, all through the
 * borrow this hands back, because a place is exactly an address plus what is stored at it.
 *
 * Bounds-checked with `assert`, so a debug build catches the mistake and a release build pays
 * nothing - the same bargain `->at()` and `string::view::byte_at` make.
 */
operator<T> (array<T>& $a)[usize $index] : T&
{
    assert($index < $a->len, "array index out of range");

    return $a->storage->at($index);
}

/**
 * The same contract over a `const` array: it reads, it chains with `->`, and it does not write.
 *
 * Not a method, so there is no receiver here to declare `const` - the operand says it, which is why
 * this is an ordinary overload rather than anything new. A mutable array still picks the form above,
 * its operand matching exactly where this one would only widen; a `const` array reaches only this.
 *
 * `const T&` is what makes it read-only all the way through: `$c[$i] = v` is then a write through a
 * const pointee, which the compiler refuses by naming exactly that.
 */
operator<T> (const array<T>& $a)[usize $index] : const T&
{
    assert($index < $a->len, "array index out of range");

    return $a->storage->at($index);
}

/**
 * The append contract: `$a[] = $value` grows by one and hands back the new slot.
 *
 * One operand where the element form has two, which is the whole of how the two are told apart -
 * they share one overload set and one name, and arity is what `AST::match_function` compares first.
 *
 * The slot it returns is **uninitialized**, and the assignment writing into it is an
 * *initialization*: there is no old value there, so none is torn down. That is the compiler's rule
 * (`AssignNode::is_initialization`), not this function's, but it is the reason this function is
 * allowed to hand back a slot it has not filled.
 */
operator<T> (array<T>& $a)[] : T&
{
    $a->reserve($a->len + 1);
    $a->len = $a->len + 1;

    return $a->storage->at($a->len - 1);
}
